        "### Insights and Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "The analysis indicates that house prices are primarily influenced by structural features, with area emerging as the most dominant factor. Additionally, the number of bedrooms, bathrooms, and the availability of amenities such as air conditioning and parking also contribute significantly to price variation. This suggests that both size and livability features play a key role in determining property value.\n",
        "\n",
        "In terms of model performance, the Linear Regression model achieved an R² score of approximately 0.65, indicating that it explains around 65% of the variance in house prices. In practical terms, the model is moderately accurate, typically predicting prices within an error range of about 9–10 lakhs. While not perfectly precise, it is reliable enough to capture overall pricing trends.\n",
        "\n",
        "A notable and somewhat unexpected finding was that the Linear Regression model outperformed the Random Forest model. This suggests that the underlying relationships in the dataset are largely linear, and that introducing more complex, non-linear models does not necessarily improve performance for this specific problem.\n",
        "\n",
        "From a business perspective, these findings highlight the importance of prioritizing property size and essential amenities when pricing houses. Real estate firms can leverage such models to establish baseline pricing quickly, while focusing on key value-driving features to remain competitive and make informed decisions.\n"
      ],
